.TH LIBMPQ__ARCHIVE_ADD_PATH 3 2026-08-18 "The MoPaQ archive library"
.SH NAME
libmpq__archive_add_path \- add a filesystem file to an MPQ archive
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <libmpq/mpq.h>
.sp
.BI "int32_t libmpq__archive_add_path("
.BI "        mpq_archive_s *" "mpq_archive,"
.BI "        const char *" "filename,"
.BI "        const char *" "source_path,"
.BI "        const mpq_file_options_s *" "options"
.BI ");"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
Reads caller-owned \fIsource_path\fP in bounded chunks and adds it under
a nonempty \fIfilename\fP. An empty source file is valid. The source file is
not modified. NULL \fIoptions\fP selects writer defaults. Archive publication
still requires
\fBlibmpq__archive_close\fP().
.PP
SINGLE storage is independent of the archive sector size. A newly oversized
unit buffers its complete declared input, is encoded/encrypted once, and remains
one logical block without a sector-offset or sector-CRC table. The new path is
limited to min(INT32_MAX, UINT32_MAX, SIZE_MAX) by current representation and
one-block reader transfer types, with additional codec and output-extent checks.
Existing inputs fitting a configured sector retain their prior behavior.
Encoding may require several times the input size in memory. There is no fixed
MiB cap or library-managed memory budget. Applications handling untrusted sizes
should apply their own resource limits. Normal allocation failures are reported
where the runtime returns control; this does not guarantee survival of OOM
termination. Existing codec/version restrictions and ADPCM SINGLE rejection,
KEY adjustments, generated listfile defaults and physical patch defaults remain.
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
/* archive is an active writer. input/sample.txt is a local test file. */
int32_t
add_local_sample(mpq_archive_s *archive)
{
    return libmpq__archive_add_path(
        archive, "docs/sample.txt", "input/sample.txt", NULL
    );
}
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
Returns \fB0\fP after the member is staged in the writer
archive, or a negative libmpq error.
.SH ERRORS
A NULL or empty member filename returns \fBLIBMPQ_ERROR_EXIST\fP before
the source is opened. An inaccessible source returns \fBLIBMPQ_ERROR_OPEN\fP.
Source read or seek failures return \fBLIBMPQ_ERROR_READ\fP or
\fBLIBMPQ_ERROR_SEEK\fP. Invalid writer state/options, duplicate names,
size limits, allocation, and output I/O errors may also propagate.
